<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP Dependency Check suppressions.

  RULES FOR THIS FILE — read before adding an entry:

  1. A suppression is a statement that a REPORTED vulnerability does not apply
     to this project, OR that it applies but has no published fix yet. It is
     not a way to make a red build green. If a fixed release EXISTS, upgrade
     the dependency instead — always.

  2. Every entry MUST carry:
       - the CVE id,
       - a <notes> explaining WHY it is suppressed (which code path is absent,
         or why no upgrade is available),
       - an expiry date in the notes, no more than 90 days out.

  3. An undated suppression is a permanently disabled gate. Re-review on expiry;
     if the CVE still has no fix, re-date it deliberately rather than leaving it
     open forever.

  4. NEVER raise failBuildOnCVSS in pom.xml to clear a finding. That disables
     the gate for every dependency, forever, invisibly. This file at least makes
     each exemption reviewable in a diff.

  5. "The version range looks wrong" is NOT grounds for suppression on its own.
     A cautionary tale from this project: the Tomcat findings were initially
     read as CPE range artifacts because the advisories also list an 11.x
     branch. Once an NVD API key was configured, the gate printed the matched
     ranges and they were unambiguous — >=10.1.0 <10.1.58 genuinely covered the
     shipped version. They were REAL vulnerabilities. Half of them were then
     FIXED by raising tomcat.version to 10.1.57; only the half with no
     published fix are suppressed below.

  TWO CATEGORIES OF ENTRY EXIST HERE — they are not equivalent:
    (A) VULNERABLE COMPONENT ABSENT — the affected module is not on the
        classpath at all. Low risk, verified by inspection.
    (B) NO FIXED RELEASE PUBLISHED — the CVE genuinely applies and there is
        real residual risk. These are ACCEPTED RISK, not dismissals, and each
        one names the version that will fix it. Treat category (B) as an open
        security item on the project, not as closed paperwork.
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- CATEGORY A — VULNERABLE COMPONENT ABSENT FROM THIS BUILD             -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->

  <!--
    CVE-2026-40974 — Spring Boot Cassandra auto-configuration does not perform
    hostname verification on the SSL connection to Cassandra.

    NOT APPLICABLE — the vulnerable component is absent: there is no Cassandra
    driver, no spring-boot-starter-data-cassandra, and no Cassandra
    auto-configuration anywhere in this build. The only datastore is PostgreSQL
    via spring-boot-starter-data-jpa with the org.postgresql driver. The finding
    arises from CPE matching at the spring-boot artifact level, not from the
    affected module being present.

    Re-review by: 2026-11-27 (90 days). If Cassandra is ever added, this entry
    MUST be removed and the finding re-assessed before wiring a cluster.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      CATEGORY A (component absent). No Cassandra driver, starter, or
      auto-configuration in this build (PostgreSQL/JPA only), so the vulnerable
      code path cannot be reached. Re-review by 2026-11-27.
    ]]></notes>
    <cve>CVE-2026-40974</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    CVE-2026-41855 — deserialization of untrusted data in
    org.springframework.jms.support.converter.MappingJackson2MessageConverter
    (and the Jackson-based JMS converters) in an untrusted JMS environment.

    NOT APPLICABLE — the vulnerable component is absent: this project has no
    spring-jms and no jakarta.jms dependency, no message listener, no broker
    connection, and no MappingJackson2MessageConverter bean. Verified by search
    across the source tree. The vulnerable classes are not on the classpath.
    The finding is reported against spring-core/spring-web because the CPE match
    is at the "spring framework" product level rather than the affected module.

    Re-review by: 2026-11-27 (90 days). If messaging is ever introduced, remove
    this entry FIRST and re-assess before connecting a broker.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      CATEGORY A (component absent). No JMS in this project: no
      spring-jms/jakarta.jms dependency, no message converters or listeners, no
      broker. The vulnerable class is absent from the classpath.
      Re-review by 2026-11-27.
    ]]></notes>
    <cve>CVE-2026-41855</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- CATEGORY B — ACCEPTED RISK: NO FIXED RELEASE PUBLISHED YET           -->
  <!--                                                                      -->
  <!-- These four CVEs GENUINELY APPLY to the embedded Tomcat this service  -->
  <!-- runs on. They are suppressed ONLY because no fixed artifact is       -->
  <!-- available to upgrade to, not because they are harmless.              -->
  <!--                                                                      -->
  <!-- EVIDENCE (printed by the dependency gate against an authenticated    -->
  <!-- NVD feed — these are correctly-matched ranges, not CPE artifacts):   -->
  <!--   CVE-2026-65905 (9.8) >=10.1.0  <10.1.58   DIGEST auth bypass       -->
  <!--   CVE-2026-65637 (9.8) >=10.1.53 <10.1.58   improper input handling  -->
  <!--   CVE-2026-68525 (9.1) >=10.1.0  <10.1.58   FORM authz bypass        -->
  <!--   CVE-2026-65182 (9.1) >=10.1.0  <10.1.58   security constraint      -->
  <!--                                             bypass                   -->
  <!--                                                                      -->
  <!-- WHY NOT FIXED: every one of these needs tomcat-embed-core 10.1.58.   -->
  <!-- 10.1.58 is TAGGED IN THE APACHE GIT REPO BUT HAS NOT BEEN PUBLISHED  -->
  <!-- TO MAVEN CENTRAL. Setting it fails the build outright:               -->
  <!--   Could not find artifact                                            -->
  <!--   org.apache.tomcat.embed:tomcat-embed-core:jar:10.1.58 in central   -->
  <!-- A git tag is cut before artifacts are staged and released.           -->
  <!--                                                                      -->
  <!-- WHAT WAS FIXED INSTEAD: tomcat.version was raised to 10.1.57 (the    -->
  <!-- highest version actually on Central), which genuinely REMEDIATES     -->
  <!-- CVE-2026-59084, CVE-2026-59083, CVE-2026-55276 and CVE-2026-53434    -->
  <!-- (ranges <=10.1.56 / <10.1.56). Those are NOT suppressed — they are   -->
  <!-- fixed, and no entry for them exists in this file.                    -->
  <!--                                                                      -->
  <!-- COMPENSATING CONTROLS while this risk is accepted:                   -->
  <!--   - The app does NOT use Tomcat DIGEST or FORM authentication. All   -->
  <!--     authentication is stateless JWT enforced by a Spring Security    -->
  <!--     filter chain (JwtAuthenticationFilter), which runs INSIDE the    -->
  <!--     servlet container and is not bypassed by a Tomcat realm/         -->
  <!--     security-constraint flaw. No <security-constraint> or <login-    -->
  <!--     config> is declared anywhere in this project — authorization is  -->
  <!--     entirely Spring Security's.                                      -->
  <!--   - Tomcat is not internet-facing: nginx terminates and proxies, and -->
  <!--     the security group exposes only 80/443.                          -->
  <!--   These reduce, but do not eliminate, exposure. This remains an OPEN -->
  <!--   SECURITY ITEM.                                                     -->
  <!--                                                                      -->
  <!-- MANDATORY ACTION ON EXPIRY (2026-11-27) OR SOONER:                   -->
  <!--   Check https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/apache/tomcat/embed/      -->
  <!--   tomcat-embed-core/ for 10.1.58+. The moment it is there, raise     -->
  <!--   <tomcat.version> in pom.xml and DELETE ALL FOUR entries below.     -->
  <!--   Do not simply re-date them without re-checking Central.            -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->

  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      CATEGORY B (ACCEPTED RISK — no published fix). CVE-2026-65905, CVSS 9.8,
      Tomcat DIGEST authentication bypass, range >=10.1.0 <10.1.58. GENUINELY
      APPLIES to tomcat-embed-core 10.1.57. Fixed release 10.1.58 is not yet on
      Maven Central, so no upgrade exists. Compensating control: this app does
      not use Tomcat DIGEST auth; authentication is stateless JWT via Spring
      Security. Re-review by 2026-11-27 — upgrade to 10.1.58+ and DELETE this
      entry as soon as the artifact is published.
    ]]></notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.tomcat\.embed/tomcat\-embed\-core@.*$</packageUrl>
    <cve>CVE-2026-65905</cve>
  </suppress>

  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      CATEGORY B (ACCEPTED RISK — no published fix). CVE-2026-65637, CVSS 9.8,
      improper input handling, range >=10.1.53 <10.1.58. GENUINELY APPLIES to
      tomcat-embed-core 10.1.57. Fixed release 10.1.58 is not yet on Maven
      Central, so no upgrade exists. Compensating control: Tomcat is not
      internet-facing (nginx proxies; only 80/443 exposed). Re-review by
      2026-11-27 — upgrade to 10.1.58+ and DELETE this entry as soon as the
